So it would seem that JSON has a similar bug to this.  It doesn't produce code that doesn't compile, but it does produce code that loses precision.  Then using that code at runtime produces an exception.

Here's the JSON repro:
```C#
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

var deserialized = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<MyPi>("{}", SourceGenerationContext.Default.MyPi);

Console.WriteLine(deserialized);

public struct MyPi
{
    [JsonConstructor]
    public MyPi(decimal value = 3.1415926535897932384626433M) { Value = value; }
    public decimal Value { get; }
}

[JsonSerializable(typeof(MyPi), GenerationMode = JsonSourceGenerationMode.Metadata)]
internal partial class SourceGenerationContext : JsonSerializerContext { }
```

This happens because JSON produces this:
```C#
    private static global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonParameterInfoValues[] MyPiCtorParamInit()
    {
        var parameters = new global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonParameterInfoValues[1];

        parameters[0] = new()
        {
            Name = "value",
            ParameterType = typeof(decimal),
            Position = 0,
            HasDefaultValue = true,
            DefaultValue = 3.1415926535897932384626433
        };
        
        return parameters;
    }
```
`DefaultValue` here is https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/7b08680a92cbd8743b59db38e5120966a0b475aa/src/libraries/System.Text.Json/src/System/Text/Json/Serialization/Metadata/JsonParameterInfoValues.cs#L43

That syntax results in initializing the `object` value to a boxed `double` which will throw at runtime:
```
Unhandled exception. System.InvalidCastException: Unable to cast object of type 'System.Double' to type 'System.Decimal'.
   at System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonParameterInfo`1..ctor(JsonParameterInfoValues parameterInfoValues, JsonPropertyInfo`1 matchingPropertyInfo)
   at System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonPropertyInfo`1.CreateJsonParameterInfo(JsonParameterInfoValues parameterInfoValues)
   at System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonTypeInfo.ConfigureConstructorParameters()
   at System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonTypeInfo.Configure()
   at System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonTypeInfo.<EnsureConfigured>g__ConfigureSynchronized|170_0()
   at System.Text.Json.JsonSerializerOptions.GetTypeInfoInternal(Type type, Boolean ensureConfigured, Nullable`1 ensureNotNull, Boolean resolveIfMutable, Boolean fallBackToNearestAncestorType)
   at System.Text.Json.JsonSerializerOptions.GetTypeInfo(Type type)
   at SourceGenerationContext.get_MyPi() in C:\scratch\jsonDecimal\System.Text.Json.SourceGeneration\System.Text.Json.SourceGeneration.JsonSourceGenerator\SourceGenerationContext.MyPi.g.cs:line 18
   at Program.<Main>$(String[] args) in C:\scratch\jsonDecimal\Program.cs:line 4
```

This is related to this issue, which was missing this case: https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/58705

I believe the fix here, needed for both Configuration and Json is refine and share our literal formatting helper:
https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/7204fbe5f56d52dd3fc4238e33de45f532f05d50/src/libraries/System.Text.Json/gen/JsonSourceGenerator.Emitter.cs#L1354-L1403

